{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sw2nhjADME325s27TH6iTC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSeveral limitations bound the scope of our conclusions. First, the oracle experiments cover two formal logic benchmarks (FOLIO $n = 204$, ProofWriter $n = 600$) that provide gold annotations; extending this analysis to commonsense or science domains requires constructing comparable gold constraint sets for those domains. Second, seven of our eleven model-domain conditions have $n \\leq 60$, providing insufficient statistical power for confirmatory claims; power analysis shows that the minimum detectable effect exceeds 5\\,pp for these conditions (Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:power}), so they serve as regime characterization rather than definitive tests. Third, we do not compare against trained FOL extractors or cross-model extraction pipelines; our findings characterize the limitations of same-model self-extraction specifically. Fourth, all models tested range from 7B to 32B parameters; whether the capability trap persists at larger scales (70B+) remains an open question. Fifth, our extraction prompts use a single zero-shot template per domain; alternative prompting strategies (few-shot exemplars, chain-of-thought extraction, or iterative refinement) might yield higher-quality constraints, though the oracle gap suggests the bottleneck is fundamental rather than prompt-specific. Sixth, the constraint quality analysis (8.2\\% Z3 semantic discriminative rate, 39.2\\% source-trace discriminative rate) covers Qwen2.5-14B on the full FOLIO + ProofWriter dataset ($N = 804$); other model-domain combinations may exhibit different constraint characteristics. Seventh, constraint extraction uses $T_{\\text{ext}} = 0.3$ while trace sampling uses $T = 0.7$; alternative extraction temperatures might yield different constraint characteristics, though our negative results suggest the bottleneck is semantic coupling rather than extraction diversity. Eighth, StrategyQA and ARC-Challenge use the mathematical extraction prompt rather than the logical one because their reasoning structure does not involve formal predicates or implications (Appendix~\\ref{app:prompts}); a domain-specific commonsense extraction template might yield different constraint characteristics for these benchmarks. Ninth, although we prompt for FOL-style formalizations, the extracted constraints are predominantly propositional ground clauses without quantifiers (\\S\\ref{sec:constraint-extraction}); whether models capable of producing genuine quantified FOL would yield higher discriminative rates is untested. Tenth, deduplication uses syntactic equivalence, so semantically equivalent constraints with different surface forms receive separate weights, potentially inflating the apparent constraint pool without adding discriminative content. Eleventh, the GSM8K pilot ($n = 30$) is included to illustrate the constraint-absent failure regime and should not be interpreted as confirmatory evidence.\n2\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016Hb6xFUswTjtRS1U7uZuVP","session_id":"ff4131df-0538-40e9-a5d8-d0234a1df865","uuid":"e46fe1c5-4c43-4392-af61-9f7064d17c93","timestamp":"2026-05-19T04:49:51.240Z"}
