{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X94dEje6qbk566A6YV9sCQ","type":"tool_result","content":"74\t\\subsection{Remediation Analysis}\n75\t\\label{sec:remediation}\n76\t\\label{sec:interventions}\n77\t\n78\tWe test nine remediation strategies spanning six intervention dimensions (full results in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:remediation}). \\emph{Prompting}: contrastive prompting reduces the discriminative rate from 8.2\\% to 6.0\\% ($\\Delta = -1.1$\\,pp), and filtering to retain only discriminative constraints yields zero gain. \\emph{Architecture}: cross-model extraction produces 0\\% discriminative constraints ($\\Delta = 0$\\,pp). \\emph{Capability}: generational scaling with Qwen3-14B yields $\\Delta = +0.49$\\,pp (n.s.). \\emph{Verification}: Z3 feedback loops trigger UNSAT on only 0.9\\% of problems with no real accuracy impact; cross-trace UNSAT checking finds 56.4\\% of constraint pairs share zero variables, so logical verification is vacuous. \\emph{Diversity}: raising temperature to $T = 1.0$ collapses $\\Delta$ from $+4.90$\\,pp to $+0.49$\\,pp (\\S\\ref{sec:t-sensitivity}); multi-temperature sampling ($n = 204$) produces $\\Delta = 0.00$\\,pp (SC $= 57.84\\%$, SICA $= 57.84\\%$). \\emph{Filtering}: consistency filtering (retaining constraints in $\\geq 4$ traces) prunes aggressively (1.8 constraints per problem at threshold 4, 0.3 at threshold 8) and reverts to SC. All six dimensions fail. The independence bottleneck is structural: it cannot be resolved by prompt engineering, solver refinements, or diversity manipulations within the same-model self-extraction paradigm."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016Hb6xFUswTjtRS1U7uZuVP","session_id":"ff4131df-0538-40e9-a5d8-d0234a1df865","uuid":"5a3e4c0e-ce30-41b8-8753-feb971ea8bd9","timestamp":"2026-05-19T04:50:20.938Z"}
